{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe bridge has nowhere to record which tracker projects a store syncs, nor which repos each\nproject's tickets belong to. Epic `0e68-41eb-5782-4336` needs that mapping to be a property of the\n**store** rather than of any one repo, because `.bridge_state/prev_snapshot.json` is shared by every\nrepo using the store: if two repos fetched different project subsets against one shared snapshot,\neach pass would see the other's projects as vanished from the remote and diff them as deletions.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `.bridge_state/projects.json` beside the committed bridge state already there (`bindings.json`,\n`get_rotation.json`, `prev_snapshot.json` — all present on the `tickets` branch), owned by a new\n`projects_store.py` module rather than by extending `binding_store.py`. The two records have very\ndifferent write cadences, and keeping them separate means an operator editing the mapping never\nrewrites the large bindings record.\n\nRecord shape:\n\n```json\n{\"version\": 1, \"legacy_default\": \"REB\", \"projects\": {\"REB\": {\"repos\": [\"rebar\"]}}}\n```\n\nThe `projects` key set **is** the store's sync list, so there is no second list to keep in agreement.\n`legacy_default` is the project that tickets predating this epic resolve to.\n\nThis choice sits well with ADR 0060 (\"Keep bridge state committed, but make its formats cheap\"): that\nADR's constraint is per-pass churn on the tickets branch — it measured 12.79 KB per changed\n`bindings.json` version and ~4.5 MB/day for `prev_snapshot.json`. The mapping changes only on an\noperator edit, so it adds no per-pass growth at all.\n\nSurface it as `bridge projects` under the verb group established by story `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a`\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py`), with the library and MCP parity epic `2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3`\nrequires of every bridge verb. The bridge library surface is `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py` (`bridge_fsck`\nat `:75`). The MCP layer is split by mutation, not by feature: `register_read_tools`\n(`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:36`) hosts tools annotated `_ANN[\"READ_ONLY\"]` — where the existing\n`bridge_fsck` tool lives at `:259` — while `register_write_tools`\n(`src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:33`) hosts mutating tools, and `mcp_server.py:663` registers both. The\n`projects` verbs therefore split across both modules: `list` is a read tool, `set` and `remove` are\nwrite tools. Sub-verbs: `list`,\n`set <KEY> --repos a,b`, `remove <KEY>`. `set` on a key that is already present **replaces** that\nkey's repos list rather than merging into it, so the command is idempotent and a repo is removed by\nre-issuing `set` without it.\n\nThe bridge group's help is a **pinned byte-exact contract**, not generated text: the group listing\nlives in `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt` (which today names only `preview` and `sync` at lines 5-7)\nand is served verbatim by `_help.subcommand_help` (`src/rebar/_cli/_help.py:52`), while the parser\nhardcodes the verb set a second time as `metavar=\"{preview,sync}\"`\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py:52-53`). Adding a verb therefore means editing three places, not\none; omitting either of the two help sites ships a group whose own help does not mention the command\nit just gained.\n\nThe module exposes two functions so that I/O and rule are separable: `load_mapping(repo_root)` reads\nand validates the record, and `resolve_project(ticket, mapping) -> str | None` is a pure function\nover an already-loaded mapping, which keeps the tri-state rule unit-testable without a store on disk.\n\n**Read behaviour when the record is missing or bad** follows the precedent `compat.py` already sets\nfor `.store-compat.json` (`docs/migrations.md:155-165`): an **absent** record is legal and reads as an\nempty mapping with a `legacy_default` of `None`, while a **malformed** one raises rather than being\nsilently treated as absent — a corrupt record must never degrade into a permissive default. Absent is\nlegal because the record is seeded by an ensure unit that runs at init, `fsck --repair`, and MCP boot;\nuntil it does, every ticket resolves to `None` and nothing syncs, which is the fail-safe direction —\nthe bridge writes nothing rather than writing somewhere wrong.\n\n`resolve_project` implements the tri-state rule so the inbound and outbound stories consume one\nimplementation instead of each re-deriving it. The states map to concrete return values:\n\n| ticket `bridge_project` | returns | meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| key absent from state | the mapping's `legacy_default` (a `str`) | legacy ticket, predates this epic |\n| `\"\"` (explicit empty) | `None` | deliberately not synced |\n| `\"REB\"` | `\"REB\"` | sync into that project |\n\n`None` is the not-synced sentinel, so every consumer branches on a single defined value: callers\ntreat `None` as \"emit no mutation for this ticket\" and any non-`None` result as a project key safe to\ntarget. A `legacy_default` that is itself empty — a store whose backend had no configured project —\nalso yields `None`, so an unconfigured store degrades to \"nothing syncs\" rather than to a malformed\nquery.\n\n`--repos` is **required** on `set`; omitting the flag is an argument error and exits non-zero.\n`--repos \"\"` is the explicit way to store an empty list, which keeps \"no default repo\" a deliberate\nstatement rather than an accident of a forgotten flag.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/projects_store.py` (new)\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py` — the subcommand, and the hardcoded `metavar` verb set at `:52-53`\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt` — the pinned byte-exact group help listing\n- `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — the `list` read tool\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py` — the `set` and `remove` write tools\n- tests under `tests/`\n\nThis story owns the mapping module; the parent epic's file impact is updated to name\n`projects_store.py` rather than `binding_store.py`, so parent and child agree that the mapping does\nnot extend the bindings record.\n\n`_mcp_writes.py` is the one file this story shares with its sibling that adds the ticket create\narguments. The overlap is forced by the codebase's own read/write registrar split rather than chosen,\nand it is safe because that sibling `depends_on` this story: the two are strictly ordered and can\nnever be edited concurrently. Introducing a third MCP module purely to avoid the shared file would\nbreak the established registrar convention for no benefit.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's CLI row — a real CLI subprocess with a persisted-state assertion —\nbecause the mechanism spans argument parsing, the on-disk JSON record, and exit codes; an in-process\nunit test would not cross the parsing or exit-code components.\n\n- Happy path: `bridge projects set REB --repos rebar` in a fresh store, then `bridge projects list`\n  returns the entry and `.bridge_state/projects.json` on disk contains it.\n- Cross-surface contract test: an entry written through the library surface is read back byte-equal\n  through the CLI and through MCP — proving one record, not three parallel stores.\n- Failure path: `bridge projects remove NOPE` on a store without that key exits non-zero, names the\n  key, and leaves the record byte-unchanged.\n- Edge: `set REB --repos` with an empty list is accepted and stores `[]` — a project with no default\n  repo is legal, not an error.\n- Replace-not-merge: `set REB --repos a,b` followed by `set REB --repos a` stores exactly `[\"a\"]`,\n  proving the second call replaced rather than merged.\n- Pinned help: `rebar bridge` with no verb, and `rebar help bridge`, both render text naming\n  `projects`, and the argparse `metavar` matches the pinned listing — asserted against the file so a\n  future verb cannot drift the two apart silently.\n- Collateral invariant: after any mapping write, `bindings.json` and `prev_snapshot.json` hash\n  identically to before.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `bridge projects set|list|remove` exist under the bridge verb group and operate on\n      `.bridge_state/projects.json`; checked by a CLI subprocess test asserting the on-disk record\n      after each verb.\n- [ ] The library and MCP surfaces read and write the same record as the CLI; checked by a test that\n      writes through one surface and reads the identical entry through the other two.\n- [ ] `resolve_project` returns the `legacy_default` string for an absent field, `None` for an\n      explicit empty value, and the key itself for a set value, and returns `None` when the\n      `legacy_default` is itself empty; checked by a parameterized unit test asserting the exact\n      return value for all four cases.\n- [ ] `set` without `--repos` is an argument error exiting non-zero, while `--repos \"\"` stores `[]`;\n      checked by a paired CLI subprocess test.\n- [ ] An absent record reads as an empty mapping with a `None` legacy default while a malformed one\n      raises; checked by a paired test over a store with no record and one with truncated JSON.\n- [ ] `list` is registered as a read tool and `set`/`remove` as write tools; checked by asserting\n      `list` carries the READ_ONLY annotation and that `set`/`remove` are registered by\n      `register_write_tools`.\n- [ ] A mapping write leaves `bindings.json` and `prev_snapshot.json` byte-identical; checked by\n      hashing both before and after in the CLI test.\n- [ ] `remove` of an unknown key exits non-zero and mutates nothing; checked in the same test.\n- [ ] `set` on an already-present key replaces its repos list rather than merging; checked by\n      asserting the stored list equals exactly the second call's argument.\n- [ ] The pinned group help in `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt` and the parser `metavar` both name\n      `projects`; checked by a test asserting the rendered `rebar bridge` help contains the verb and\n      that the pinned file and the `metavar` verb set agree."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786161383215887001,"uuid":"5712cdb9-7b1f-464a-863e-2cc6f90894a3"}